/**
@mainpage Supervisor Unit Design Document

@section udd_what This document

This is level 5 of the V-model described in `docs/v-model-process.md`, for the
independent safety supervisor of `SWA-22`: the second microcontroller that
reads the chamber thermocouples, holds one element of the contactor coil, and
takes instruction from nothing.

**It is generated from the source and has no separate existence.** The unit
design lives in the headers beside the code it describes, because a unit design
kept in another file is a document that disagrees with the code, and the
disagreement is found by whoever is auditing rather than by whoever is editing.
What is read here was compiled.

@section udd_scope Scope, and what is deliberately outside it

| In | Out |
|---|---|
| `core/`, the trip logic, the front-end decode and the self-diagnostics | The controller (ESP32-S3), which is a separate argument and a separate document |
| `protocol/`, the wire format the supervisor transmits | `board/stm32g031.h`, 4 000 register definitions generated from ST's CMSIS-SVD, which is nobody's unit design |
| `src/`, the composition root and the cycle | The host test harness |
| `board/`, the silicon boundary | |

The supervisor is **3 488 bytes of program memory** and four source files carry
the whole safety function. That is the reason this document can be read end to
end, and the reason the architecture put the safety function on its own part.

@section udd_reading What each unit states, and why those five things

A unit entry answers the same five questions in the same order, because they
are the questions an assessment under EN ISO 13849-1:2023 cl. 4.6 or
EN IEC 60730-1 Annex H actually asks of a safety function:

- **@c \\implements** the requirement that makes the unit exist, and
  **@c \\derivedfrom** the architecture decision above it. A unit with neither
  is a unit nobody asked for.
- **@c \\param** and **@c \\retval** with **@c \\range**: what may be passed and
  what comes back, in units and bounds rather than in types alone. `uint32_t`
  is a type; "milliseconds since the previous call, saturating" is an interface.
- **@c \\errorbehaviour**: what happens when the input is wrong. On a safety
  function this is not an edge case, it is most of the specification.
- **@c \\rationale**: why it is this way and not the obvious way. This is the
  one that gets left out and the first one an assessor asks about, because a
  decision with no recorded reason has to be re-derived by whoever reviews it,
  and re-derivation is where a safety argument quietly changes.
- **@c \\verifiedby**: the test that covers it. Every unit here is exercised on
  a development host with no hardware, which is `SWA-01` applied to the
  component whose whole argument is that it can be verified independently.

@section udd_safety What this component is, in safety terms

The supervisor exists so that the loss of the controller, by any means, does
not become the loss of protection. It is single-channel and holds **one series
element** of the contactor coil: it can refuse heat on its own and cannot
command it on its own.

@warning **No performance level and no SIL is claimed.** `docs/safety.md`
states the position and this document does not improve on it. What is claimed
is narrower and checkable: a documented language subset, the diagnostics listed
below, MC/DC over the trip logic, and an architecture that keeps the safety
function on a part the network cannot reach.

The diagnostics of EN IEC 60730-1 Annex H that this component implements, each
documented in @ref selfcheck.h :

| Measure | Unit |
|---|---|
| Invariable memory (program memory) | `sup_flash_ok`, CRC-32 against a digest stamped in after linking |
| Variable memory | `sup_ram_word_ok` and the walking pattern test, including an address-dependent pattern |
| Stack | A 256-word guard, filled at start-up and checked in full every cycle |
| Program sequence | Four stages that announce themselves, verified once per cycle |
| Clock | Cross-checked against the LSI through a windowed watchdog |

@section udd_trace Traceability

Three index pages carry it, each generated from the comments rather than
maintained:

- \ref implements "Requirements implemented": every unit against the `SWR-` it
  realises.
- \ref derivedfrom "Architecture traceability": every unit against the `SWA-`
  it comes from.
- \ref verifiedby "Unit verification": every unit against the test that covers
  it.

The reverse direction, requirement to source, is also enforced outside this
document: `strictdoc export` fails the build when a `@relation` marker in the
source names a requirement that does not exist.

@section udd_limits What this document does not tell you

Stated here rather than discovered later:

- **It describes units, not the system.** Whether the units compose into a
  protection that holds is `docs/safety.md` and `docs/safety-supervisor.md`.
- **It is not a test report.** @c \\verifiedby names the test; whether it
  passed is the CI run and the coverage report.
- **The transport code has never run on silicon.** The SPI bring-up, the
  chip-select timing, the SysTick period and the USART configuration are
  written and unproven, which `firmware/supervisor/README.md` says plainly and
  which no amount of documentation changes.
*/
